{"id":"aero_fields_sourcing","compound_name":"Simulation::Aero::aero_fields_sourcing","source_path":"aero_fields_sourcing/aero_fields_sourcing.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"Using fields to create interesting sources for aero.","documentation_file":"aero_fields_sourcing_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Fields with Aero Sourcing\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses fields to modify geometry data and create volumes that are inputs to a basic aero system, with the purpose of creating much more interesting sourcing for aero. \n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nThe inputs for this graph are the meshes used as sources for an aero simulation. The output is the resulting aero simulation volume.\n\n### How it works\n\n- Blue section: Starting with an animated sphere mesh, the first thing to do is to compute the velocity of the points, and then to use fields to modify this velocity data by adding some turbulence to it. The blue section of the graph outlines this process: Compute the point velocities, create a `fractal_turbulence_field` (this field outputs vectors), sample the field at the point locations, then add this turbulence to the point velocities. Finally, set this data as `point_velociy`. When this object is then input to the `source_aero`, it will already have point_velocity information and so the aero solver will not have to compute it. Since the incoming velocity information contains noise, this noise can be inherited by the aero smoke using the `inherit_velocity` property.\n\n- Red section: The sphere gets converted to a volume and noise is applied to `fog_density` before the volume gets fed into the aero simulation. Remember that the aero system accepts both meshes and volumes. Internally it always converts meshes to volumes, but this computation is skipped if the input is already a volume. The red section of the graph outlines this process: `convert_to_volume`, extract the `fog_density`, create a `fractal_noise_field` (this field outputs scalars), multiply the `fog_density` with the noise values, set the `fog_density` values back on the object.\n\nWith those two things done, the sphere with noisy `point_velocity` and `fog_density` values is input to the aero system for a much more dynamic type of source.\n\nNote that since the input volume object already has `fog_density`, the `fog_density` property on the `source_aero` node is ignored.  However, `fog_density_mode` still has an effect.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":59,"connections":95,"values":82,"compounds":6},"node_types":{"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":3,"Core::Fields::sample_field":2,"Geometry::Properties::get_point_position":2,"Core::Math::add":2,"Core::Array::first_in_array":2,"Core::Math::multiply":2,"Core::Graph::pass":2,"Core::Logic::if":2,"Core::Time::time":2,"Geometry::Properties::set_geo_property":1,"Core::Fields::fractal_turbulence_field":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Common::collider":1,"Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Aero::source_air":1,"compute_point_velocity":1,"Modeling::Converters::convert_to_volume":1,"Core::Fields::fractal_noise_field":1,"Geometry::Volume::update_voxel_position":1,"animated_sphere":1,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":1,"Rendering::Arnold::Arnold_standard_volume_mat":1,"time_to_field":1,"Simulation::Aero::post_refine_aero":1,"Geometry::Properties::set_voxel_fog_density":1,"Geometry::Properties::get_voxel_fog_density":1,"Geometry::Properties::get_voxel_position":1,"Modeling